SM(545,{"n":"1 Corinthians 12-13","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 12-13.mp3","d":2295.4,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.44,"And those members of the body which we think to be less honorable, on these we bestow greater honor, and our unpresentable parts have greater modesty."],[30.56,"But our presentable pot parts have no need."],[33.84,"So we know what he's talking about."],[36.48,"Yeah. So say St."],[37.92,"Paul is saying that the parts of ours that we don't display publicly."],[43.1,"Our body parts that we don't go and display publicly, and that we attribute modesty to."],[50.44,"It's not that those are not important parts of our body."],[53.96,"Someone could say, oh, well, you're ashamed of this part of your body, that's why you keep it covered."],[58.52,"But that doesn't make it unimportant."],[58.68,"that It kind of implies covering it is honoring it."],[65.61,"Because the fact that we're covering it and protecting it is because it's important."],[70.33,"But so the analogy being, that a lot of those, a lot of those tasks and gifts and ministries may not be the desirable ones, you know, that we're proud of."],[81.95,"When the bishop comes to visit, you know, we don't the first person we present to him is that this is the this is the man who scrubbed the toilets."],[89.31,"Uh-huh. But that doesn't mean that that job is not important."],[93.55,"But if you had a saintly member of the church, he might be the very one who volunteers to the toilets."],[99.47,"Exactly. So this might be the holiest person in the whole church because he's willing to take the job no one else wants and is humble about it and isn't going to get any glory for it."],[109.03,"He could be the most right, the most important person uh in the church."],[114.23,"Just because he's doing those jobs that we don't we don't uh think are very honorable."],[119.19,"But God composed the body, having given greater honor to that part which lacks it, that there should be no schism in the body, but that the members should have the same care for one another."],[130.71,"So he's saying, again with the analogy of body parts, but the person in the church who's doing things and isn't getting the honor for it before other human beings, that's the person who God honors to compensate for that."],[144.41,"The person who's up in front."],[146.43,"Who everyone say, oh, you know, that person is getting their reward"],[150.71,"The person who's off unthanked."],[154.36,"That's the person who God is going to honor and reward and thank uh for what they've done, so that it's evened out."],[162.22,"And when he says that the members should have the same care for one another, he's saying that we should look at each other in that same way that God looks at it."],[173.92,"So when we see the person in the church who's working hard and is really using their gifts and doing these things and not getting any credit for it, that we would thank them and honor them for it."],[189.14,"As well. Lo atok it at. it the same way God looks instead of the way we've been culturally taught to think, oh, this is what's really important"],[197.34,"Is this the first germ of the idea of equality among people?"],[203.74,"Because we were talking about slavery and it only gets attacked in a Christian context."],[210.86,"Yes. Yeah, because this is very much against the prevailing culture."],[218.7,"Yes. Yes. And that this is part of why St."],[222.14,"Paul is saying you can't bring those things into the church."],[226.62,"Because in the church, everyone is equal before God."],[231.26,"They're not all the same, but they're all equal."],[234.98,"We're all different."],[236.68,"But we're all on the same standing."],[237.96,"We all have the same responsibility to use the gifts God has given us to help everyone."],[244.53,"And everyone using their gifts is worthy of honor."],[249.91,"And if we don't give it to them, then God will."],[252.95,"Um, yes. So that's very much, very much what what this is about."],[258.07,"Uh, we're gonna see how that plays out coming up here ethically."],[263.53,"In terms of morality and ethics, which is gonna be very different."],[267.93,"Because, you know, uh Plato's Republic, since we already talked about Plato a little, morality sort of boils down to a version of give to everyone their due."],[278.09,"Mm-hmm. And so certain people, certain superior people are do more, and certain inferior people are do less."],[287.65,"And society should be organized so that the people on the bottom stay on the bottom, and the people on the top are allowed to stay on the top because that's where they belong, in both cases."],[299.51,"And and that sort of thinking."],[301.43,"And so there's going to be a very different understanding of ethics and morality within the church bas,ed on the idea of the equality of all of all human beings."],[312.15,"And of course, St."],[313.11,"Paul is getting this from Christ Hsimelf."],[315.43,"Christ Himself, who, when God, when Yahweh, the God of Israel, who created the heavens and the earth, created everything. when he chooses to enter into his creation, it's not just that he's born as a little helpless baby"],[329.17,"It's that he's born as a little helpless baby to peasants who are below dirt what we think of today as dirt poor, who are legally non persons, non persona under Roman law, who lived his life basically homeless and then was tortured to death by the government."],[352.05,"And the military with the religious leaders looking on."],[355.82,"So that's who God is for Saint Paul."],[359.02,"And so that idea transforms how he's going to look at a person who's a slave, who Christ died for."],[365.26,"And the way he looks at a incredibly wealthy person who Christ died for, and everyone in between."],[372.18,"That's it's gonna then this, what he's teaching here in First Corinthians and his other epistles is going to literally transform the world and produce the world we live in now to a large extent, where we take these things for granted."],[387.78,"We at least assume that they're true, whether we practice the way."],[391.38,"Yeah. We take for granted the idea."],[393.58,"The idea that that certain things are wrong, you know, and certain things are right, and people are equal."],[399.42,"You know, if someone came along teaching what Aristotle taught, that womanhood is a birth defect."],[406.11,"We'd look at them like a crazy person."],[408.91,"And we'd say, 'You're a crazed misogynist, go home, go home, you're drunk'"],[415.45,"But that was great wisdom in the ancient world."],[420.26,"Oh, Aristotle explained why women are the way they are."],[423.14,"It's a birth defect."],[424.6,"They thought that was like brilliant."],[427.46,"So we but we just take for granted these kind of ideas."],[430.98,"But yes, St."],[431.86,"Paul, what St."],[433.3,"Paul is preaching, this is the first time humanity has heard this."],[438.9,"And that's why there are so many women and slaves becoming Christians."],[442.9,"Because they're finally being told you're a you're hu f as fully human as anyone else, and God loves you."],[449.46,"God loves you so much, that he died for you."],[452.12,"for you And now this is what that God wants in your life."],[452.44,"And they sign on"],[463.52,"Right. In a way that it doesn't hit people today."],[466.72,"And so verse 26 if one member suffers, all the members suffer with it."],[473.44,"Or if one member is honored, all the members rejoice with it."],[477.52,"And this is still the metaphor."],[479.3,"Like the hangnail you mentioned."],[481.22,"Toe's not a very important part of your body."],[484.03,"We would say, but if you get a hangnail, it's not just like, oh, my toe is suffering, but I'm fine."],[490.67,"It's the whole body."],[492.41,"Suffers with it."],[493.85,"And, you know, if the honored example would be, you know, if somebody comes up and gives you a back or a shoulder rub."],[501.1,"You don't say, Oh, my back feels good, the rest of me still is right."],[506.38,"Or if you eat a good meal, you don't just say, Oh, my taste buds are really enjoying this."],[511.04,"But but you all participate."],[513.36,"And this is a segue into what he's about to talk about, because this is an important point he's just made about the relationship between the members of the community and the members of Christ."],[526.48,"Because while that's true for a human body, that part also."],[532.1,"Just like everyone honoring each other isn't necessarily true."],[536.38,"So also everyone really rejoicing when one person is honored and weeping with the one person who suffers, unfortunately, isn't always true."],[548.98,"Because as sinful humans, you know, when one person gets honored, we get jealous."],[554.66,"We go, well, why them and not me?"],[556.82,"I did this and that."],[557.7,"And don't they know that they didn't."],[559.51,"Gossip starts, jealousy starts, envy starts, all these things start."],[563.67,"And, you know, unfortunately, sometimes when one person suffers, the other people say, Well, oh well, thank God it wasn't me."],[572.21,"Or, oh, did you hear what happened to them and just right talk about it, blame them for it, you know?"],[578.45,"Oh, I knew they shouldn't have, you know, you know."],[581.73,"And that that is not how it can be."],[583.41,"Um, if we're really, if we're really one body"],[589.82,"So now he's St."],[590.86,"Paul's going to continue."],[592.3,"Verse 27. Now you are the body of Christ and members individually."],[596.06,"So that's a summary."],[597.74,"But what does it mean?"],[599.18,"I mean, we're so used to this, because this isn't just an analogy."],[602.72,"This is the result of the analogy."],[604.64,"The analogy wasn't your community is like the body of Christ."],[609.44,"What he said was, you are Christ, you're like a body."],[614.24,"Okay. So when he says you're the body of Christ, what does that mean?"],[618.16,"Well, he talked about already, he used that energies idea that God is working in the world."],[624.16,"The way that Christ was present in the world during his earthly ministry was how?"],[629.64,"That was his human body."],[632.3,"That's how God was present with his people."],[635.5,"Now Christ isn't gone somewhere, but we don't see him bodily in front of us."],[644.3,"We talked about this when we were going through St."],[646.14,"John's Gospel, where he talked about I'm still gonna be with you, but I'm gonna be with you in a different way."],[652.55,"You won't you won't see me visibly the same way, but the Holy Spirit will come and he'll make me present among you, and he'll reveal me."],[661.11,"So we see him when we see each other."],[662.97,"That's it. That's why we say Christ is in our midst when we're in worship."],[665.95,"The Holy Spirit makes him present with us."],[669.55,"So when he says you're the body of Christ."],[670.83,"the He's saying the way that Christ is now present in world, is now seen in the world, is through you."],[679.91,"Not you individually, you as a community."],[683.4,"You collectively are the body of Christ."],[686.2,"The community is the body of Christ in Corinth."],[689.72,"The way they're going to see Christ in Corinth, the way they're going to interact with Christ in Corinth is going to be through this community."],[695.96,"So that's for Lafayette, Louisiana, that's us."],[701.77,"That's how people are going to see Christ."],[703.53,"That's how they're going to interact with Christ."],[705.37,"That's how they're going to know who Christ is."],[707.55,"Collectively. And then individually, we're parts of that body."],[712.35,"Who each have our own purpose in doing that?"],[715.75,"Our own part to play, our own our own piece."],[721.83,"Verse 28, and God has appointed these in the church, first apostles, second prophets, third teachers."],[728.47,"After that, miracles, then gifts of healings, helps, and ministrations, varieties of tongues."],[735.75,"Okay, so he lit he again gives another list."],[738.53,"Now these are more ministries."],[740.39,"Before he listed gifts, now these are ministries."],[743.59,"A lot of them involve the same kind of things."],[746.64,"Yeah, this list is very parallel."],[748.72,"Yeah. And so again, tongues is the same kind of thing here."],[754.1,"He says, Are all apostles or all prophets?"],[755.54,"or Are all teachers all workers of miracles?"],[755.7,"all Do interpret?"],[759.34,"Do all have gifts of healings?"],[761.02,"Do all speak with tongues?"],[763.66,"But earnestly desire the best gifts, and yet I show you a more excellent way."],[769.74,"So this is all still summary."],[772.68,"So he's picking up those gifts he talked about before."],[775.88,"By the way, sometimes people ask me, because we get prophets talked about here."],[780.52,"Remember there were some prophets in Acts?"],[784.1,"Where would that correspond in the list of gifts?"],[788.74,"He talked about those who have the word of wisdom and those who have the word of knowledge."],[793.12,"And here he talks about prophets, prophets, and teachers."],[797.2,"teachers. So would correspond to knowledge"],[798.92,"Okay. Prophets would correspond to wisdom."],[806.37,"So the prophets who we still have among us would be, for example, monastic elders."],[813.11,"People who are spiritually advanced and have wisdom from God to offer us advice."],[818.79,"And that's why we have collected the sayings of the desert fathers from the fourth and fifth century."],[825.49,"All the way down to today."],[828.23,"That that and so uh and that's not just that's not just an analogy."],[835.43,"Um I know I wasn't here when I was going through the old testament."],[839.27,"Um but but all through the old testament there are all of these parallels."],[845.03,"So for example, there are communities of prophets."],[848.07,"Yeah in the old test yeah, in the old testament."],[850.15,"There are prophets living in communities separate from right, there are prophets who live as hermits like all like St."],[855.83,"Elias, Elijah. And so the monasticism is the continuation of the way of life of the prophets of the Old Testament, with with Saint John the forerunner, Saint John the Baptist is sort of the hinge point."],[870.29,"Where sort of the Old Testament prophethood ends and monasticism begins."],[876.22,"That that sort of hinge point."],[878.22,"So that's the institution that Saint Paul is referring to."],[883.42,"The term Monacos has not been invented yet."],[886.7,"So he's still using the prophet terminology"],[891.52,"But he has in mind people like Agabus, who we saw in Acts, but who is are following a pattern that goes to Saint John the Forerunner and then back into the back into the prophets."],[904.96,"So that's who the prophets are talking about."],[908.74,"And so that's interesting because usually the way history is usually taught is that the monasticism appears after Constantine because there are you can't get martyred anymore, so you go out and martyr yourself."],[925.38,"And I've always been suspicious of that."],[927.5,"But uh When Christianity was a persecuted religion, people who are living this sort of life were still living it within the general Christian community because the whole Christian community was separate."],[941.02,"I mean, St."],[941.74,"Paul's telling the whole community of Corinth they have to basically separate themselves into a separate independent community."],[948.94,"So that's sort of a monastic community already."],[953.34,"So after Christianity is legalized, that's why you have monasticism as a separate institution arise then, because sort of not everyone's now living monastically."],[966.79,"At that point."],[967.83,"Before that, essentially everyone was, and that's why when you when you read documents from the the first through the the third centuries of the church, there's so much emphasis on chastity and virginity and that kind of thing, and you think, well, did they think marriage was bad and they only decided marriage was good after Constantine?"],[987.31,"Well, no, it's because of the way the Christian way of life was sort of quasi monastic under persecution, for that entire period, and so you see more emphasis on those monastic virtues just across the board because of the lifestyle that everyone is living at that time."],[1005.81,"Yeah, so there's a continuity there, it's not just something that suddenly pops up, and that's also why it's not coincidental that we see the prophets and the apostles, the apostles. are also in this category"],[1020.49,"But we see the the we see the prophets in the Old Testament are the ones who have the vision of God."],[1026.43,"And have these sort of direct spiritual experiences."],[1029.39,"And in our own Orthodox monastic tradition, we have these instances of monastics receiving the vision of Christ in uncreated glory receiving these same kind of visions."],[1041.47,"Again, because there's this continuity, in the in the way of life."],[1047.11,"But St. Paul's being clear, not everyone is called to that."],[1051.28,"Not everyone is a prophet."],[1053.2,"And never was in the Old Testament."],[1056.02,"And isn't now in uh Corinth and isn' todtay."],[1061.06,"So this is at one particular calling amongst others"],[1065.98,"Uh and uh Saint Paul already has that idea clearly in the 50s AD."],[1072.48,"And so he said, not everyone does any of these one things, just like the bot whole body isn't made up of eyes."],[1078.9,"That's uh this is the practical effect."],[1081.46,"So chapter beginning of chapter 13, 13, verse 1."],[1086.18,"Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I have become sounding brass or a clanging cymbal."],[1093.7,"Um so I'll pause here momentarily, because this is generally the verse that people who want to argue for the uh nonsense sounds as speaking ingu tugs want to point to because they want to talk about oh, see the the lanage of angels."],[1110.46,"Oh my gosh."],[1114.8,"Yeah, that that is somehow the langu language of angels."],[1118.08,"So there's two possible understandings of this."],[1121.6,"I'll give the one that I don't buy first."],[1124.16,"Uh the one that I don't buy is that angels should here be translated as messengers, which is what the word actually means."],[1129.84,"Oh and of course, messengers from different places would speak different languages."],[1134.56,"I think that's unlikely though, because it's contrasted with men, and most messengers would have been men."],[1142.24,"So men and messengers doesn't really doesn't really make sense."],[1146.96,"I think it is far more likely that this is referring uh to both some of the Old Testament prophetic literature and a lot of the Second Temple Jewish literature that uh apocalypses where people prophets will take journeys to heaven and into the underworld and see things and have these things revealed to them and have these discussions with with angelic beings."],[1174.37,"And see all these things."],[1175.73,"And often, and you see this picked up in the book of Revelation in the New Testament, they will see things that they're told not to reveal."],[1184.55,"They'll have these secrets, these sort of heavenly secrets revealed to them, and they'll be you can't don't write this down."],[1190.33,"Don't write this part down, or don't don't tell anyone this, or you know, you wrote it down, seal the book."],[1196.87,"Until the end of days or something."],[1198.85,"That kind of that kind of theme."],[1201.33,"And I think that is most likely what St."],[1204.37,"Paul is referring to here."],[1206.61,"Not that the angels in heaven have some particular spoken language that someone can learn."],[1213.89,"Because every time we see an angel appear and talk to somebody, they don't need a translator in the scriptures."],[1220.71,"And even in those writings."],[1222.47,"I think that is what it's referring to."],[1224.33,"That even if I know all the languages in the world."],[1226.99,"He starts where he ended."],[1228.27,"If I know all the languages in the world, and I even, you know, know these mysterious things from you know the third heaven."],[1238.84,"Uh, but I don't have love, then I'm just a sounding brass or clanging symbol."],[1243.89,"As opposed to speaking which communicates, which is another reason why this isn't some weird babbling angelic language."],[1252.27,"I'm just making noise."],[1253.63,"That's what a symbol is."],[1255.25,"So if the tongues of angels was making a bunch of noises, this wouldn't make a lot of sense."],[1260.79,"He's contrasting this with just making senseless noise, saying something sensible."],[1266.23,"And though I have the gift of prophecy and understand all mysteries and all knowledge, again, here's what prophecy is."],[1274.41,"What is prophecy?"],[1275.37,"Not predicting the future."],[1277.22,"It's understanding all mysteries."],[1280.24,"And all knowledge."],[1281.36,"And though I have all faith, again, this is faithfulness, so that I could remove mountains."],[1285.92,"That clear very clearly is probably a reference to Christ's saying, you know, if you have faith, aside Muslims feet, you can say this mountain, go throw yourself in the sea, and it would do it."],[1296.32,"But have not love, I am nothing."],[1298.72,"And though I bestow all my goods to feed the poor, and though I give my body to be burned, but have not love, it profits me nothing."],[1306.02,"So these second parts are all for a reason."],[1309.96,"The it profits me nothing."],[1312.06,"This is again, seems to be an obvious allusion to Christ's teaching about uh having treasures in heaven."],[1320.07,"Don't build up for yourself treasures on earth, treasures in heaven."],[1321.03,"The idea being I would go and give up all I have, and then my reward would be in heaven"],[1327.79,"And he's saying, But if I do that, but I do it without love, there's no reward rate."],[1332.77,"There's no there's no profit to be had."],[1334.53,"And he's saying, even if I give my body to be burned, even if I'm a martyr, but I don't have love, it doesn't mean anything."],[1342.36,"There's no reward."],[1343.4,"There's no reward for me."],[1345.0,"Um the reason I'm emphasizing these places where it seems like he's referring to Christ's teaching, since I've been doing that tonight, is that there are some scholars who like to try to, because St."],[1359.16,"Paul wrote before the Gospels, they want to not accept the Gospels. as being historically accurate at all"],[1367.92,"Right? So they want to be able to say, oh well, Christ didn't really say that stuff that the Gospels say he said."],[1374.88,"The Gospel writers made it up and put the words in Christ's mouth."],[1379.36,"Well, if St."],[1380.24,"Paul, who's writing this early, is referencing these things that Christ said, that's pretty good evidence."],[1388.02,"That St. Paul knew that he had said them, meaning before the Gospels were written down, the church already was communicating these things that Christ said and already knew them, meaning they actually go back to Christ Himself."],[1402.43,"Which is exactly what we see here."],[1404.69,"But that creates a big problem for them."],[1406.77,"That's also the reason I've been pointing out where St."],[1408.61,"Paul does use the name Jesus, because they like to point to that too"],[1413.35,"And say, oh, see, the Gospels are about Jesus."],[1416.63,"St. Paul's just talking about Christ."],[1418.31,"This is two different things."],[1420.49,"They're trying to separate and make all these disjunctions in the New Testament."],[1426.01,"But if you read carefully at all, I find it difficult to believe that St."],[1432.11,"Paul just coincidentally chooses these examples we just read."],[1435.87,"Especially the connection between faith and moving a mountain."],[1439.95,"That he just pulled that out of his hat."],[1441.79,"And it just so happens that a couple decades later, gospel writers pulled the same rabbit out of a different hat."],[1450.92,"At random. And I find it equally unlikely that the gospel writers went back to St"],[1458.04,"Paul's epistles and took just pulled these lines out and constructed whole stories around them in a different order."],[1465.48,"Neither of those makes any sense."],[1473.0,"St. Paul is referencing them here, and eventually they get written down after being passed around orally in the churches."],[1481.4,"But what is what is St."],[1482.84,"Paul's theme here?"],[1484.44,"Not just that love is more important."],[1487.53,"Love for each other within the community is more important than any of the gifts, but that love is what gives all of these gifts meaning and purpose and reality."],[1500.63,"That without love they're nothing."],[1503.24,"So love is the necessary precondition."],[1507.42,"Love is what unites the body together."],[1509.9,"And love is the necessary precondition for any of these things, any of these gifts having any meaning or any honor or any value."],[1518.86,"Is because they're they're being practiced out of love."],[1522.38,"So now in verse 4, he's gonna define what he means by love."],[1526.94,"Love suffers long and is kind."],[1529.42,"Love does not envy."],[1531.06,"Love does not parade itself, is not puffed up, does not behave rudely, does not seek its own, is not provoked, thinks no evil, does not rejoice in iniquity, but rejoices in the truth, bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things, and I'll go a little bit into verse eight."],[1552.98,"Love never fails."],[1554.9,"Now, unfortunately, that gets quoted all the time at weddings."],[1562.9,"Because as we very much like to do in our society, we like to boil uh love down to romance."],[1569.75,"That's what it's all about is romance."],[1572.29,"Here's the problem with using this at a wedding."],[1575.81,"The kind of love that is usually celebrated at most weddings in American culture fails."],[1582.13,"Seems to quite often."],[1584.09,"At least half the time, according to statistics."],[1587.45,"And it doesn't endure all things."],[1589.85,"It doesn't hope all things."],[1591.31,"This is not talking about romance."],[1594.37,"This is talking about the love that unites Christ to his people, Christ's people to Christ, and Christ's people to each other."],[1604.13,"So what does that mean love is doing?"],[1606.37,"Love is doing for Saint Paul the same thing that the Eucharist does for St."],[1611.01,"Paul. What the Eucharist does is a ritual, as a celebration, uniting the people together and uniting them to Christ is also what love does."],[1621.51,"Love is the constituent of it."],[1624.88,"Because what is what is happening in the Eucharist when St."],[1628.04,"Paul talked about it."],[1629.0,"It's the manifestation of Christ's love, his sacrifice of himself."],[1634.06,"For our sakes."],[1635.76,"This is what love is."],[1638.0,"St. Paul said in Romans that Christ died for us while we were still sinners."],[1641.68,"The distinctive thing about all of these is that they are unselfish."],[1646.16,"Yes. They're not referring to the self at all."],[1648.96,"Yes. And so love, is not a feeling, that we have toward people."],[1659.21,"Oh, I just love. it"],[1660.49,"It's nice when I hear people say, I just love my church."],[1663.45,"I love the people in my church."],[1665.47,"But it's got to go deeper than. that"],[1669.35,"There's an old rabbinic Jewish story about this rabbi who went to sit down at a lunch counter, and the man next to him was eating a fish."],[1677.11,"And he said, Man, I love this fish."],[1680.15,"The rabbi said, You love the fish."],[1682.47,"You killed him and you're eating it."],[1688.23,"The fish isn't feeling the love."],[1691.0,"So the point being, what he meant was not, I love this fish as another being in the world."],[1700.06,"As another one of God's creatures."],[1701.82,"What he meant was, I love the taste of this fish."],[1704.86,"I love what this fish is doing for me."],[1707.74,"I like love how eating this fish makes me feel."],[1711.62,"And a lot of the times when we say we love people, even when we say we love our church and love our community, we're talking about I love how I feel when I come here."],[1721.94,"I love how I feel when I'm around these people, they make me happy."],[1726.5,"That's not the kind of love St."],[1728.26,"Paul is talking about."],[1729.54,"One of the interesting things that we don't think about in the scriptures is that love is a commandment in the scriptures."],[1738.74,"When they come to Christ and say, What is the first and the greatest commandment?"],[1745.88,"And he gives two."],[1747.8,"One from Deuteronomy and one from Leviticus."],[1751.41,"The first one is love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind and with all your strength."],[1755.89,"Commandment two love your neighbor as yourself"],[1757.37,"Commandment. So love is something we're commanded to do."],[1764.73,"We're told to do, meaning it's something that takes effort."],[1768.49,"It's something that requires effort."],[1770.57,"And again, we live in our world of quote unquote romantic love."],[1775.85,"No one in the scriptures fell in love with a man or a woman and decided to get married."],[1782.01,"Didn't happen. They were all married off at around the age of 12 or 13 to someone they might or might not have ever met before."],[1791.14,"Now you're married."],[1792.82,"And St. Paul says, Husbands, love your wives, and by the way, love her the way Christ loved the church."],[1799.38,"Love her so much you die for her."],[1801.74,"And he doesn't say if you like how she looks."],[1808.94,"He doesn't say if you like her personality."],[1812.62,"You need to do it."],[1814.96,"And so that's another thing we take for granted."],[1817.6,"And so that's the same commandment that St."],[1819.76,"Paul is giving the Corinthians here."],[1822.0,"You need to love each other."],[1823.52,"He's already given us a hint of what that means."],[1826.32,"That was if one of you is in pain or suffering, that all of you are there with them."],[1832.9,"Suffering with them, weeping with them."],[1835.62,"And if one of you is celebrating some success or some victory, all of you are there celebrating with that person."],[1843.54,"And that's part and parcel of what he expands on here"],[1850.06,"Because what are the things that would stop you from doing that?"],[1853.98,"What would stop you from celebrating with someone who's rejoicing?"],[1857.82,"Envy. Envy, jealousy, your own pride."],[1861.9,"Those are all the things he attacks here."],[1864.94,"And so this is something we're being commanded to do."],[1869.18,"It's not him saying, Oh, how one like we read it at weddings, oh, how wonderful love is."],[1875.26,"Isn't love grand?"],[1876.72,"That's not well, you also can't command a person to feel a certain way."],[1882.25,"I mean, you could you you can tell someone that maybe they ought to, and there are the reasons why, but you can't say uh you feel happy."],[1887.69,"Yeah Not happy."],[1895.65,"And and then turn it on."],[1897.41,"I mean, I just won't happen."],[1900.43,"Yeah. And so it's not about how you feel."],[1903.15,"Anybody who's been married for any length of time knows this."],[1907.23,"No, honestly. Sometimes you really feel it."],[1911.31,"You really feel feel love."],[1914.37,"For your spouse."],[1915.41,"Hopefully often. But no one feels it always."],[1919.27,"There are days and moments when you're not feeling it."],[1923.27,"That doesn't mean you stop loving them because you don't feel it."],[1928.39,"Unfortunately, in our culture and society today, it does."],[1931.63,"People have one too many days of not feeling it, and so they get divorced."],[1936.43,"But that's not that's not how it's to work."],[1940.21,"It's when you're not feeling it that it takes a little more effort."],[1946.22,"And the same is true, the same is true in the community of the church that St."],[1950.78,"Paul's speaking to now."],[1953.66,"We all have people, who are fellow preachers and fellow church members who it's real easy for us to love because most of the time we feel it."],[1961.74,"We enjoy being with them, we enjoy spending time with them."],[1965.48,"And so loving them, not a big problem, most of the time."],[1969.4,"Even them, there will be moments."],[1971.58,"There' be momllents where things happen."],[1974.03,"We all also have people in the church who it's really hard almost all the time."],[1981.65,"Where for whatever reason our personalities don't fit, don't rub the right way."],[1987.41,"Everything you try to say, they take the wrong way."],[1990.13,"Everything they say, you take the worst possible."],[1992.47,"It's just constant."],[1993.75,"And it takes a lot of effort."],[1995.35,"But in neither of those cases do you have a different commandment than what St."],[2000.15,"Paul is giving here."],[2001.29,"To love each other."],[2002.89,"To love each other."],[2004.25,"Because that's what's going to make everything that you do as a member of the community worthwhile and valuable and real."],[2012.83,"Because you're doing it with love."],[2014.51,"And you're doing it out of love."],[2015.63,"And so that's why he's saying this is the most important thing"],[2019.51,"And this is the thing that if it's not there, that it doesn't matter how good your church programs are, and it doesn't matter how good the chanting is, and it doesn't matter how beautiful the choir is, and it doesn't matter how how good the priest is as a liturgist or a preacher, and it doesn't matter how much money you have people donating."],[2038.23,"None of that matters if you don't love each other."],[2040.87,"And if you do love each other and the choir's off key and the chanter gets lost, gets lost somewhere, and the priest is off key and forgets half his sermon halfway through preaching it, and all that stuff is a mess, but you love each other, then it's all worthwhile."],[2060.47,"'s That's what he saying, because then Christ is there."],[2064.64,"And you're united to him."],[2066.72,"So St. Paul continues in verse 8: Love never fails."],[2070.72,"But whether there are prophecies, they will fail."],[2074.08,"Whether there are tongues, they will cease."],[2076.48,"Whether there is knowledge, it will vanish away."],[2079.12,"For we know in part and we prophesy in part."],[2082.24,"But when that which is perfect has come, then that which is in part will be done away."],[2086.56,"So what's he talking about?"],[2088.64,"Well, what is the perfect thing that comes?"],[2091.04,"Well, that would be Christ."],[2092.24,"The kingdom, when Christ returns."],[2093.92,"So as much as I hate to admit it."],[2095.7,"When someday I die, andor Christ returns, all my Bible studying isn't gonna be worth much."],[2104.26,"People probably haven't thought about it."],[2104.9,"We're not gonna have the Bible in the new heavens and the new earth because we're not gonna need it"],[2111.19,"Good point. And he's saying we're not gonna need prophecy and wisdom."],[2115.99,"We're not gonna need to translate languages."],[2118.63,"All that's gonna all that's eventually gonna go away."],[2121.99,"That's all stuff for this world."],[2123.93,"That's all stuff for this life."],[2125.69,"It has an expiration date, which makes it different than love."],[2130.65,"Because that's not gonna go away when Christ returns."],[2134.17,"The love that connects us to each other and to Christ, that's there forever."],[2139.13,"Verse 11 When I was a child, I spoke as a child, I understood as a child, I thought as a child."],[2145.05,"But when I became a man, I put away childish things."],[2148.09,"For now we see in a mirror dimly, but then face to face"],[2153.57,"Now I know in part, but then I shall know, just as I also am known."],[2159.25,"So he's he compares sort of these things in this life to when he was a child, when he was a kid."],[2166.45,"And we've all run into precocious kids who say cute things."],[2169.71,"About why the sky is blue or about unicorns, you know, and it's cute and it's funny because they're a kid."],[2175.39,"And they kind of partially know things."],[2178.44,"And then they sort of come to weird conclusions that we think are funny when they try to piece together the rest that they don't know."],[2184.3,"He's saying, What someday when we're in the new heavens of the new earth, that's how we're gonna look back at this life and all the things we thought we understood, and all the things we thought we figured out, we'll look at it and think it's kind of funny and quaint because we'll see the reality."],[2199.56,"We'll know the reality of who of who Christ is."],[2203.18,"We'll we'll know him face to face."],[2205.68,"And so the other analogy he he says is now we see in a mirror dimly."],[2210.41,"We kind of see a reflection."],[2213.15,"But then we'll see we'll see fully, we'll see Christ face to face."],[2217.79,"And he says, Now I know in part."],[2220.99,"Now, this is someone who's seen Christ repeatedly, but even he says, Now I know in part, but then I'll know fully, because I'll know Christ the way he knows me when that day comes."],[2232.99,"He says, and now abide in faith, hope and, love."],[2237.19,"Abide meaning live, dwell."],[2239.53,"These are the things that should carry drives your life."],[2241.85,"Faithfulness, hope, looking forward to that day when Christ returns, and love for each other."],[2248.65,"He says, but the greatest of these is love."],[2251.37,"And love is the most important one."],[2253.29,"Because again, faithfulness is something for this life."],[2256.33,"Hope is looking forward to the world to come."],[2259.45,"Love is going to be the constant all the way through."],[2264.09,"So this is a good place to end for the evening."],[2268.89,"So thank you, everybody."],[2272.97,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2279.93,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com"],[2285.73,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.44,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 12:23-24 - Unpresentable parts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>less honorable</b> members get <b>greater honor</b>; unpresentable parts have <b>greater modesty</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Parts we don't display publicly</li><li class=\"d1\">not <b>unimportant</b> - covering = <b>honoring</b> it, protecting it because it matters</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: tasks and gifts we're not proud of</li><li class=\"d1\">bishop visits - don't present \"the man who scrubbed the toilets\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but the <b>saintly member</b> may be the one who volunteers for the toilets</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe the <b>holiest person</b> in the church - takes the job no one wants, humble, no glory</li><li class=\"d1\">\"could be the most important person in the church\"</li></ul>"},{"t":119.19,"lv":3,"h":"12:24-25 - Honor to the part that lacks it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God composed the body: <b>greater honor to the part that lacks it</b> → <b>no schism</b>, same care for one another</li><li class=\"d0\">Person in front gets their reward now; person <b>unthanked</b> → God honors and thanks them, \"evened out\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Same care</b> = look at each other as God does</li><li class=\"d1\">thank and honor the one working hard with no credit</li><li class=\"d1\">not by what we're <b>culturally taught</b> is important</li></ul>"},{"t":197.34,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: equality among people","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: first germ of the idea of <b>equality</b>? slavery only attacked in a Christian context</li><li class=\"d0\">Yes - very much against the <b>prevailing culture</b>; why Paul says you can't bring those things into the church</li><li class=\"d0\">In church all <b>equal before God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not all the <b>same</b> - all different, but same standing</li><li class=\"d1\">same responsibility to use gifts to help everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone using gifts is <b>worthy of honor</b> - \"if we don't give it, God will\"</li></ul>"},{"t":258.07,"lv":3,"h":"Plato's justice vs Christian ethics","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Coming up: <b>ethics and morality</b> very different</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Plato's <i>Republic</i></b>: morality = <b>give everyone their due</b></li><li class=\"d1\">superior people do more, inferior do less</li><li class=\"d1\">society organized so bottom <b>stays on bottom</b>, top stays on top - \"where they belong\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Church: ethics based on <b>equality of all human beings</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul gets this from <b>Christ Himself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Yahweh enters creation as a helpless baby to peasants <b>below dirt poor</b>, legally <b><i>non persona</i></b> under Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">lived basically <b>homeless</b>; <b>tortured to death</b> by government and military, religious leaders looking on</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's who God is for St. Paul\"</li></ul>"},{"t":359.02,"lv":3,"h":"How Paul's teaching transformed the world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Changes how he sees a <b>slave</b> Christ died for - and the <b>wealthy</b>, and everyone between</li><li class=\"d0\">1 Corinthians and other epistles will <b>transform the world</b> → produce the world we live in</li><li class=\"d1\">we take for granted things are right/wrong, people <b>equal</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Aristotle</b>: womanhood is a <b>birth defect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">today: \"crazed misogynist, go home, you're drunk\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in ancient world = \"great wisdom\", \"brilliant\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's message the <b>first time humanity heard</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">why so many <b>women and slaves</b> became Christians</li><li class=\"d1\">told: fully human, <b>God loves you</b>, died for you - \"they sign on\"</li></ul>"},{"t":466.72,"lv":2,"h":"12:26 - One suffers, all suffer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 26</b>: one member suffers, all suffer; one honored, all rejoice</li><li class=\"d0\">Still the metaphor - the <b>hangnail</b></li><li class=\"d1\">toe not important, but the <b>whole body</b> suffers</li><li class=\"d0\">Honor: a shoulder rub - not \"my back feels good, rest of me fine\"</li><li class=\"d1\">good meal - not just \"my taste buds enjoy this\"; <b>all participate</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Segue to what's next: relation of <b>members of the community</b> to members of Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Not automatic among sinful humans</li><li class=\"d1\">one honored → <b>jealousy</b>, \"why them, not me?\", gossip, envy</li><li class=\"d1\">one suffers → \"thank God it wasn't me\", \"I knew they shouldn't have\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't be so if we're <b>really one body</b></li></ul>"},{"t":589.82,"lv":2,"h":"12:27 - You are the body of Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 27</b>: \"Now you are the body of Christ and members individually\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not an analogy but its <b>result</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"community is like the body of Christ\" - he said <b>\"you are\"</b> Christ's body</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ's earthly presence = his <b>human body</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now not gone, but <b>not seen bodily</b> - as in John's Gospel: Holy Spirit makes me present</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we see him when we see each other\" → <b>Christ in our midst</b> in worship</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Collectively</b> = the body, not individually</li><li class=\"d1\">Corinth sees and interacts with Christ through this community - \"for Lafayette, Louisiana, that's us\"</li><li class=\"d1\">individually, parts with our own purpose, own piece</li></ul>"},{"t":721.83,"lv":2,"h":"12:28-31 - Ministries, and prophets as wisdom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 28</b>: apostles, prophets, teachers, miracles, healings, helps, ministrations, tongues</li><li class=\"d1\">a list of <b>ministries</b> (before: gifts) - parallel; tongues same kind of thing</li><li class=\"d0\">Are all apostles? all prophets? all speak in tongues? → <b>earnestly desire the best gifts</b>; \"more excellent way\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: where do <b>prophets</b> fit in the gift list?</li><li class=\"d1\">teachers ↔ <b>knowledge</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophets ↔ <b>wisdom</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Prophets still among us = <b>monastic elders</b></li><li class=\"d1\">spiritually advanced, wisdom to advise us</li><li class=\"d1\">why we collected the <b>sayings of the desert fathers</b>, 4th-5th c. to today</li></ul>"},{"t":835.43,"lv":3,"h":"Prophets and monasticism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Old Testament parallels: <b>communities of prophets</b>; prophets as <b>hermits</b> like <b>Elijah</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Monasticism</b> = continuation of prophets' way of life</li><li class=\"d1\">St. <b>John the Baptist</b> = \"hinge point\" where OT prophethood ends, monasticism begins</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul uses <i>prophet</i> - term <i>monachos</i> not yet invented</li><li class=\"d1\">has in mind people like <b>Agabus</b> in Acts</li><li class=\"d0\">Usual history: monasticism after <b>Constantine</b> - no martyrdom, so \"martyr yourself\" - \"always suspicious\"</li><li class=\"d1\">before, whole community already <b>separate</b>; Paul tells Corinth to separate = already \"quasi monastic\"</li><li class=\"d1\">after legalization not everyone lives monastically → separate institution</li><li class=\"d0\">Explains 1st-3rd c. emphasis on <b>chastity and virginity</b> - not \"marriage was bad\"; lifestyle under persecution</li></ul>"},{"t":1005.81,"lv":3,"h":"Prophets' vision of God; not everyone called","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Continuity: OT prophets have the <b>vision of God</b>, direct spiritual experiences</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox monastics receive <b>vision of Christ in uncreated glory</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul clear: <b>not everyone</b> is called to that</li><li class=\"d1\">not everyone a prophet - never in OT, not in Corinth, not today</li><li class=\"d1\">one particular calling among others - Paul has this idea in the <b>50s AD</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not all one thing - \"whole body isn't made up of <b>eyes</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1081.46,"lv":2,"h":"13:1 - Tongues of men and of angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>13:1</b>: tongues of men and angels, no love = <b>sounding brass, clanging cymbal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Verse tongues-as-nonsense people cite: \"language of angels\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Two readings</li><li class=\"d1\">one he doesn't buy: <i>angeloi</i> = <b>messengers</b> from different places, different languages</li><li class=\"d1\">unlikely - contrasted with <b>men</b>, and messengers were men</li><li class=\"d0\">Likely: <b>OT prophetic and Second Temple apocalypses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophets journey to heaven and underworld, discuss with angelic beings</li><li class=\"d1\">Revelation: told <b>not to reveal</b> secrets, \"seal the book until end of days\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not a heavenly spoken language - angels in Scripture <b>never need a translator</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1222.47,"lv":3,"h":"Cymbal = noise, not communication","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Even if I know all languages <b>and</b> mysteries from the <b>third heaven</b>, no love → just brass or cymbal</li><li class=\"d0\">Contrast: speaking that <b>communicates</b> vs \"just making noise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">tongues of angels as babbling wouldn't make sense of the contrast</li></ul>"},{"t":1266.23,"lv":2,"h":"13:2-3 - Prophecy, faith, giving it all","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 2</b>: prophecy, all mysteries and knowledge, <b>all faith</b> to remove mountains, no love = <b>nothing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Prophecy = not predicting future; <b>understanding all mysteries and knowledge</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Faith = <b>faithfulness</b>; mountain = Christ's saying</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 3</b>: give goods to the poor, give body to be burned, no love → <b>profits me nothing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">allusion to <b>treasures in heaven</b> teaching</li><li class=\"d1\">give up all for heavenly reward - without love <b>no reward</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even <b>martyrdom</b> means nothing without love</li></ul>"},{"t":1345.0,"lv":3,"h":"Paul echoes Christ before the Gospels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pointing out Paul's references to <b>Christ's teaching</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some scholars: Paul wrote <b>before the Gospels</b> → Gospels not historically accurate</li><li class=\"d1\">claim: Gospel writers <b>made it up</b>, put words in Christ's mouth</li><li class=\"d0\">But Paul writing early references things Christ said</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul knew he said them; church communicated them <b>before Gospels</b> → go back to <b>Christ Himself</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Also why he notes where Paul uses name <b>Jesus</b> - they try to separate \"Jesus\" and \"Christ\"</li><li class=\"d1\">unlikely Paul pulled \"faith moves mountain\" <b>out of his hat</b> and Gospels did same</li><li class=\"d1\">equally unlikely Gospel writers built stories from Paul's lines in different order</li><li class=\"d1\">rather: passed around <b>orally</b>, later written down</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's theme: love is <b>precondition</b> giving gifts meaning; <b>unites the body</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1522.38,"lv":2,"h":"13:4-7 - Love defined","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 4-7</b>: suffers long, kind; doesn't envy, parade, puff up, behave rudely, seek its own, provoked, think evil; rejoices in <b>truth</b>; bears, believes, hopes, endures <b>all things</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 8</b>: \"Love never fails\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Quoted at <b>weddings</b> - we boil love down to <b>romance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wedding-style love <b>fails</b> - \"at least half the time, according to statistics\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't endure or hope all things</li><li class=\"d0\">Not romance: love that unites <b>Christ to his people</b> and people to each other</li><li class=\"d0\">Love does for Paul what the <b>Eucharist</b> does</li><li class=\"d1\">ritual uniting people to Christ and each other; love is the <b>constituent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Eucharist = Christ's love, <b>sacrifice of himself</b>; Romans: died for us <b>while still sinners</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Distinctive: all <b>unselfish</b>, no reference to the self</li></ul>"},{"t":1648.96,"lv":3,"h":"Love is not a feeling - the fish story","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Love not a <b>feeling</b> toward people; \"I just love my church\" must go deeper</li><li class=\"d0\">Rabbinic story: man at lunch counter eating fish - \"I love this fish\"</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbi: \"You killed him and you're eating it\" - <b>\"the fish isn't feeling the love\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">meant: I love the <b>taste</b>, what it does for me</li><li class=\"d0\">Often \"I love my church\" = <b>I love how I feel</b> around these people, they make me happy</li><li class=\"d1\">not Paul's kind of love</li></ul>"},{"t":1729.54,"lv":2,"h":"Love is a commandment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Love is <b>commanded</b>: first and greatest commandment</li><li class=\"d1\">Deuteronomy: love the Lord with <b>heart, soul, mind, strength</b>; Leviticus: love neighbor as yourself</li><li class=\"d0\">Told to do → takes <b>effort</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Our world of \"<b>romantic love</b>\" - no one in Scripture fell in love and decided to marry</li><li class=\"d1\">married off ~<b>12 or 13</b> to someone they may never have met</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>husbands, love your wives</b> as Christ loved the church - die for her</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"if you like how she looks\" or her personality - \"you need to do it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same commandment to Corinthians: love each other</li><li class=\"d1\">hint: if one suffers all there weeping; one succeeds, all celebrating</li><li class=\"d1\">obstacles: <b>envy, jealousy, pride</b> - what he attacks here</li></ul>"},{"t":1875.26,"lv":3,"h":"You can't command a feeling","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not \"isn't love grand?\" - can't command someone to <b>feel</b> a way</li><li class=\"d0\">Not about how you feel - anyone married knows</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes you feel it, hopefully often; <b>no one always</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not feeling it ≠ stop loving</li><li class=\"d1\">our culture: a few days of not feeling it → <b>divorce</b></li><li class=\"d0\">When you're not feeling it, it takes <b>more effort</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same in the church</li></ul>"},{"t":1953.66,"lv":3,"h":"Easy and hard people to love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Some fellow members easy to love - enjoy their time, but even then <b>moments</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Some hard almost all the time</li><li class=\"d1\">personalities rub wrong; everything you say they take wrong, and vice versa</li><li class=\"d1\">\"takes a lot of effort\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Neither case: a different commandment - <b>love each other</b></li><li class=\"d0\">That makes everything you do as a member <b>worthwhile and real</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2019.51,"lv":3,"h":"Programs don't matter without love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't matter how good <b>programs, chanting, choir, priest as liturgist or preacher, donations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">None of that matters if you don't <b>love each other</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If you love: choir off key, chanter lost, priest forgets half his sermon, a mess → <b>all worthwhile</b></li><li class=\"d1\">because <b>Christ is there</b>, you're united to him</li></ul>"},{"t":2066.72,"lv":2,"h":"13:8-10 - Love never fails","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 8</b>: prophecies fail, tongues cease, knowledge vanishes; we know and prophesy <b>in part</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 10</b>: \"that which is perfect\" = <b>Christ</b>, the kingdom at his return</li><li class=\"d0\">\"All my Bible studying isn't gonna be worth much\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no Bible in <b>new heavens and new earth</b> - won't need it</li><li class=\"d1\">no prophecy, wisdom, translating languages</li><li class=\"d0\">Stuff for this life - <b>expiration date</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Love doesn't go away - connects us to each other and Christ <b>forever</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2139.13,"lv":3,"h":"13:11-12 - Child, mirror, face to face","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 11</b>: as a child I spoke, understood, thought as a child; as a man put away childish things</li><li class=\"d1\">precocious kids: why sky is blue, unicorns - partial knowledge, funny conclusions</li><li class=\"d1\">someday we'll look back at this life as \"<b>funny and quaint</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 12</b>: now a <b>mirror dimly</b>, then <b>face to face</b>; know in part, then know as I am known</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul has seen Christ repeatedly, still \"I know in part\"</li><li class=\"d1\">then I'll know Christ the way he knows me</li></ul>"},{"t":2232.99,"lv":2,"h":"13:13 - Faith, hope, love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 13</b>: now abide <b>faith, hope, love</b>; greatest is love</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Abide</i> = live, dwell - what should drive your life</li><li class=\"d1\">faithfulness</li><li class=\"d1\">hope = looking forward to Christ's return</li><li class=\"d1\">love for each other</li><li class=\"d0\">Why love greatest</li><li class=\"d1\">faith is for <b>this life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hope looks to the <b>world to come</b></li><li class=\"d1\">love is the <b>constant all the way through</b></li></ul>"}]});
